<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Matsya</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 350 BCE (approx.) · capital Viratanagara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small early kingdom in eastern Rajasthan, remembered in the Mahabharata as the land where the Pandavas spent their year in hiding.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Matsyas appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a> and later Vedic texts as a people west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kurus</a>. By the 6th century BCE they formed one of the sixteen mahajanapadas in Buddhist lists, with their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairat">Viratanagara</a>, modern Bairat near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known about its government or society. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virata">Virata</a> of Matsya shelters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandava</a> brothers in disguise during their thirteenth year of exile, and tradition derived the capital's name from him. The country lay between the Aravalli hills and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and seems to have been politically weak, at times dominated by its neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Matsya was absorbed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> in the 4th century BCE. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairat">Bairat</a> had a Buddhist monastery, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhabru-edict">Bhabru edict</a>, addressed to the Buddhist community, was found nearby. The area later passed through many hands before becoming part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/matsya.jpg" alt="Matsya" width="320" height="449"><figcaption>Image: Anant Shivaji Desai, Ravi Varma Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsya_Avatar_of_Vishnu_Printed_by_Ravi_Varma_Press_-_Vintage_Poster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 350 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Viratanagara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Matsya Kingdom, Maccha Mahajanapada</dd></dl></aside></div>

